# HR360 — Human Resource Management & Analytics System

## DOCUMENT CONTROL

**Document ID:** HR360-GOV-004
**Version:** 1.0
**Status:** Active
**Date:** 20 September 2026
**Document Owner:** Nida Khan
**Project:** HR360

---

# 1. Purpose

The purpose of this Document Control standard is to establish a consistent approach for creating, naming, reviewing, approving, versioning and maintaining HR360 project documentation.

The control process ensures that project documents remain traceable, current and aligned with approved requirements.

---

# 2. Documentation Repository

All HR360 project documentation will be maintained within:

```text
HR360/
└── documentation/
```

The documentation repository is organized by project discipline.

```text
documentation/
│
├── 01_Project_Governance/
├── 02_Business_Analysis/
├── 03_Functional_Requirements/
├── 04_Backend/
├── 05_Frontend/
├── 06_Analytics_BI/
├── 07_Agile_Scrum/
├── 08_Testing_UAT/
└── 09_Project_Closure/
```

---

# 3. Documentation Structure

| Folder                     | Purpose                                              |
| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| 01_Project_Governance      | Charter, stakeholders, RACI and governance           |
| 02_Business_Analysis       | BRD, business rules and process analysis             |
| 03_Functional_Requirements | FRD, user stories, acceptance criteria and RTM       |
| 04_Backend                 | API, database, ERD and backend process documentation |
| 05_Frontend                | UI, screens, navigation and dashboard requirements   |
| 06_Analytics_BI            | KPIs, analytics and Power BI documentation           |
| 07_Agile_Scrum             | Epics, backlog, sprint and Scrum documentation       |
| 08_Testing_UAT             | Test scenarios, UAT and defect management            |
| 09_Project_Closure         | Final RTM, deployment and project summary            |

---

# 4. Document Identification Standard

Each controlled document should have:

* Document ID
* Document title
* Version
* Status
* Date
* Author
* Document owner
* Review/approval information where applicable

Document IDs will follow the pattern:

```text
HR360-[AREA]-[NUMBER]
```

Examples:

```text
HR360-GOV-001
HR360-GOV-002
HR360-BA-001
HR360-FR-001
HR360-BE-001
HR360-FE-001
HR360-BI-001
HR360-UAT-001
```

---

# 5. Version Control Standard

The project will use controlled document versions.

| Version | Meaning                                           |
| ------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| 0.x     | Working draft / internal development              |
| 1.0     | First approved baseline                           |
| 1.1     | Minor update with no major requirement change     |
| 1.2     | Additional minor revision                         |
| 2.0     | Major approved change affecting document baseline |

Example:

```text
BRD v0.1 → Initial working draft
BRD v0.2 → BA review update
BRD v0.3 → Stakeholder review update
BRD v1.0 → Approved baseline
BRD v1.1 → Minor approved change
BRD v2.0 → Major scope revision
```

---

# 6. Document Status

The following statuses will be used:

| Status           | Meaning                                    |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Draft            | Document is under preparation              |
| Draft for Review | Document is ready for stakeholder review   |
| Under Review     | Formal review is in progress               |
| Approved         | Document has been accepted/baselined       |
| Superseded       | Replaced by a newer approved version       |
| Archived         | Retained for historical/reference purposes |

---

# 7. Document Ownership

| Responsibility                  | Assigned To                                   |
| ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Project documentation ownership | Nida Khan                                     |
| Business analysis documentation | Nida Khan                                     |
| Technical documentation         | Nida Khan                                     |
| Requirements traceability       | Nida Khan                                     |
| Document version management     | Nida Khan                                     |
| Business approval               | Product Owner / Relevant Business Stakeholder |
| Technical review                | Nida Khan                                     |
| UAT documentation approval      | Relevant Business Stakeholder                 |

---

# 8. Review and Approval Process

Controlled documents will generally follow:

```text
Create
  ↓
Internal Review
  ↓
Business / Technical Review
  ↓
Updates
  ↓
Approval
  ↓
Baseline
  ↓
Controlled Change Management
```

A document should not be treated as an approved baseline until the appropriate stakeholder has reviewed and accepted it.

---

# 9. Change Control

Changes to approved documentation should be traceable.

A material change should include:

* Change description
* Reason for change
* Requestor
* Impact assessment
* Affected requirements
* Affected documents
* Approval decision
* New document version

Where applicable, the change should also be reflected in:

* Product backlog
* User stories
* Acceptance criteria
* RTM
* Test scenarios
* Process maps
* Technical implementation

---

# 10. File Naming Convention

Files should follow a consistent naming standard.

Recommended format:

```text
HR360_[Document_Name]_v[Version]
```

Examples:

```text
HR360_Project_Charter_v1.0.docx
HR360_Stakeholder_Register_v1.0.docx
HR360_RACI_Matrix_v1.0.docx
HR360_BRD_v1.0.docx
HR360_FRD_v1.0.docx
```

Avoid:

```text
final.docx
final2.docx
latest.docx
new_final.docx
updated_final_v3.docx
```

This prevents ambiguity regarding the current baseline.

---

# 11. Document Review Frequency

| Document Type          | Review Trigger                   |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Project Charter        | Major scope/governance change    |
| Stakeholder Register   | New stakeholder or role change   |
| RACI Matrix            | Responsibility change            |
| BRD                    | Requirement or scope change      |
| FRD                    | Functional requirement change    |
| Business Rules         | Business-rule change             |
| Process Maps           | Process change                   |
| API Documentation      | API contract/change              |
| Database Documentation | Schema change                    |
| UI Requirements        | UI/process change                |
| KPI Definitions        | KPI/business reporting change    |
| Test Documentation     | Test scope or requirement change |
| UAT Documentation      | UAT cycle/update                 |

---

# 12. Document Baseline

The following documents form the initial governance baseline:

| Document ID   | Document             | Version | Status           |
| ------------- | -------------------- | ------: | ---------------- |
| HR360-GOV-001 | Project Charter      |     1.0 | Draft for Review |
| HR360-GOV-002 | Stakeholder Register |     1.0 | Draft for Review |
| HR360-GOV-003 | RACI Matrix          |     1.0 | Draft for Review |
| HR360-GOV-004 | Document Control     |     1.0 | Active           |

Future approved documents will be added to the controlled baseline.

---

# 13. Source Code and Documentation Traceability

Project documentation and source code should remain aligned.

Where a requirement results in a software change, traceability should be maintained through:

```text
Business Requirement
        ↓
Functional Requirement
        ↓
User Story
        ↓
Acceptance Criteria
        ↓
Technical Implementation
        ↓
Test Scenario
        ↓
UAT
        ↓
Release
```

Git/GitHub commits should be used to provide additional implementation traceability.

---

# 14. Document Storage and Version Control

The project documentation will be stored within the HR360 project repository.

Source-code changes will be controlled through Git/GitHub.

Documentation changes should also be committed to source control where appropriate.

Example:

```text
git add documentation/
git commit -m "Add HR360 project governance documentation"
git push origin master
```

---

# 15. Document Security

Project documents may contain business-process information and should be treated as controlled project information.

The following should not be stored in documentation:

* Database passwords
* API secrets
* `.env` credentials
* Authentication tokens
* Private keys
* Sensitive personal employee information

Actual credentials must remain outside version-controlled documentation.

---

# 16. Document Control Responsibilities

Nida Khan is responsible for maintaining document consistency across the project.

The Product Owner and relevant business stakeholders are responsible for reviewing and approving business-facing documents within their areas of authority.

Technical documentation should be maintained alongside implementation changes.

---

# 17. Document Control Principles

HR360 documentation will follow these principles:

1. One controlled source for each document.
2. Clear document ownership.
3. Consistent naming.
4. Version tracking.
5. Review and approval before baselining.
6. Traceability of material changes.
7. No credentials or secrets in documentation.
8. Alignment between requirements, implementation and testing.
9. Historical versions retained where required.
10. Superseded documents clearly identified.

